---
import DocsLayout from "../../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro";
import { Code } from "astro-expressive-code/components";
---

<DocsLayout title="TOTP (2FA)">
  <h1>TOTP (2FA)</h1>
  <p>
    TOTP is a second factor on top of password (or OAuth) login. Identity
    follows RFC 6238: 30-second window, SHA-1, 6 digits, ±1 step skew.
    Secrets are encrypted at rest with AES-GCM using
    <code>GATEWAY_TOTP_ENCRYPTION_KEY</code>.
  </p>

  <h2>Configuration</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <code>GATEWAY_TOTP_ENCRYPTION_KEY</code> — base64-encoded 32-byte key.
      <strong>Required in production.</strong> Without it the container
      uses a fixed dev key and logs <code>using_dev_totp_encryption_key</code>.
    </li>
    <li>
      <code>GATEWAY_TOTP_RECOVERY_PEPPER</code> — base64-encoded value that
      must decode to <strong>at least 32 bytes</strong>; used as the
      HMAC-SHA-256 key for hashing and verifying recovery codes.
      <strong>Required whenever <code>GATEWAY_TOTP_ENCRYPTION_KEY</code> is
      set</strong> — the server hard-fails on boot if the encryption key is
      present but the pepper is missing or too short. Without it, recovery
      codes cannot be hashed or verified.
    </li>
    <li>
      <code>GATEWAY_TOTP_ISSUER</code> — string shown next to the account
      in user authenticator apps.
    </li>
    <li>
      <code>GATEWAY_LOGIN_CHALLENGE_EXPIRY_SECONDS</code> (default 300) —
      how long after a successful password check the user has to complete
      the TOTP step.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <Code code={`# Generate a fresh 32-byte key
openssl rand -base64 32`} lang="bash" />

  <h2>Enrollment</h2>
  <p>The user must be authenticated. Two-step ceremony:</p>

  <h3>1. Begin setup</h3>
  <Code code={`curl -sX POST http://localhost/identity.v1.IdentityService/BeginTotpSetup \\
  -H "Authorization: Bearer $TOKEN" \\
  -H 'Content-Type: application/json' -d '{}'`} lang="bash" />
  <Code code={`{
  "secret":         "JBSWY3DPEHPK3PXP",
  "qrCodeUri":      "otpauth://totp/Acme:alice@example.com?secret=...&issuer=Acme",
  "recoveryCodes":  ["MFRGGZDFMZ", "NBSWY3DPEB", ...]
}`} lang="json" />
  <p>
    The client encodes <code>qr_code_uri</code> as a QR code for the user
    to scan. <strong>The recovery codes are shown once.</strong> Save them
    — there is no read-back endpoint.
  </p>

  <h3>2. Verify and enable</h3>
  <p>
    Enrollment is not active until the user proves they scanned the secret
    correctly:
  </p>
  <Code code={`curl -sX POST http://localhost/identity.v1.IdentityService/VerifyTotpSetup \\
  -H "Authorization: Bearer $TOKEN" \\
  -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \\
  -d '{"code":"123456"}'`} lang="bash" />
  <p>
    On success, the user's <code>totp_required</code> flag flips to
    <code>true</code>, and subsequent <code>PasswordLogin</code> calls
    return a <code>login_challenge_id</code>.
  </p>

  <h2>Login with TOTP</h2>
  <Code code={`# 1. Password check returns a challenge instead of tokens.
LOGIN=$(curl -sX POST http://localhost/identity.v1.IdentityService/PasswordLogin \\
  -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \\
  -d '{"email":"alice@example.com","password":"correct horse battery"}')

CHALLENGE=$(echo "$LOGIN" | jq -r .loginChallengeId)

# 2. Submit the 6-digit TOTP (or a recovery code).
curl -sX POST http://localhost/identity.v1.IdentityService/VerifyTotp \\
  -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \\
  -d "{\\"loginChallengeId\\":\\"$CHALLENGE\\",\\"code\\":\\"123456\\"}"`} lang="bash" />
  <p>
    The challenge is single-use and expires after
    <code>GATEWAY_LOGIN_CHALLENGE_EXPIRY_SECONDS</code>. Submitting a
    recovery code consumes it (it cannot be reused).
  </p>

  <h2>Recovery codes</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>10 codes are issued on enrollment.</li>
    <li>Each is a one-time use; consumed codes are crossed off in the DB.</li>
    <li><code>RegenerateRecoveryCodes</code> issues a fresh batch of 10 (requires password confirmation) and invalidates the old ones.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Disabling TOTP</h2>
  <p>
    <code>DisableTotp</code> requires the user's password as
    confirmation. On success, the encrypted secret is deleted, all
    recovery codes are voided, and the user's <code>totp_required</code>
    flag flips back to <code>false</code>.
  </p>
</DocsLayout>
